# Debian packaging

This directory provides debhelper-based packaging for native amd64 and arm64
builds. Application source files and the existing bundle scripts are unchanged.

## Build

Install the native library and packaging Build-Depends listed in
debian/control. Rust and Cargo 1.96 or newer and Bun must be available in PATH.

For tools installed through rustup or standalone installers, use the external
toolchain profile:

```sh
export PATH="$HOME/.bun/bin:$PATH"
dpkg-checkbuilddeps -Ppkg.waku.external-toolchain
dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc -Ppkg.waku.external-toolchain
```

This profile excludes only the cargo, rustc, and bun Debian package
dependencies. All other package dependencies remain checked. The build always
runs debian/check-build-tools to verify the actual executables and the Rust
and Cargo minimum versions before compiling. It does not install or download
tools. The Bun directory above is the standalone installer's default; adjust
PATH if Bun is installed elsewhere.

When all three tools are installed as Debian packages, the default build is:

```sh
dpkg-checkbuilddeps
dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc
```

The build invokes scripts/bundle-linux.sh with a separate CARGO_TARGET_DIR
under debian/cargo-target. It preserves the existing target directory and the
dev watcher. Native builds only are supported because the bundle assembler
selects SDK artifacts for the build host. The upstream version in
`debian/changelog` must match Cargo.toml; map a SemVer prerelease separator
`-` to `~` in the Debian version.

The resulting `waku_<version>-<revision>_<architecture>.deb`, `.changes`, and
`.buildinfo` files are written to the parent directory by dpkg-buildpackage.
Build in a disposable checkout if that output location is not appropriate.

## Install and update

```sh
sudo apt install ../waku_0.1.20-1_amd64.deb
```

Use the arm64 package on arm64 systems. Waku and its private helpers retain
their bin/share layout under /usr/lib/waku. The package provides /usr/bin/waku
and installs the desktop entry and icon under /usr/share. The tarball
self-update marker is excluded, so updates must be installed through APT or
another package manager. No APT repository is configured.

A user-local tarball installation may shadow the system package in PATH or
the applications menu. Remove that installation before switching to the
Debian package. Removing or purging this package does not remove ~/.waku.

## Validation

Run the build-tool regression test without building or installing Waku:

```sh
debian/tests/build-tools
```

It uses an isolated PATH to check minimum versions, prereleases, missing
tools, malformed version output, and executable failures.

The superficial autopkgtest checks the installed binaries, helper resources,
launcher symlink, desktop entry, and absence of the self-update marker. It
does not open a window or exercise provider integrations. It can also inspect
an extracted package without installing it:

```sh
dpkg-deb -x ../waku_0.1.20-1_amd64.deb /tmp/waku-package-test
WAKU_TEST_ROOT=/tmp/waku-package-test debian/tests/package-layout
```

## Distribution limits

The existing build downloads Cargo dependencies, a pinned Cua SDK, and its
source, and builds the SDK with Waku's existing host extension. This packaging
supports local binary builds; it does not make the upstream build offline or
suitable for upload to the Debian archive. A complete dependency license and
source audit is still required before redistribution through that archive.

`${shlibs:Depends}` is generated from the packaged ELF files. Dependencies
loaded at runtime by the graphics/windowing stack and the desktop portal are
also declared explicitly. Build on the oldest distribution intended to be
supported: newer build hosts can raise the minimum library versions. A working
graphics driver and a suitable desktop portal backend remain necessary.
